[08:44:48] {"type":"tool_end","payload":{"toolCallId":"chatcmpl-tool-a5452cf3f8918674","toolName":"read","result":"<file path=\"C:\\IA Trainings\\CalcService\\context-pack\\system\\execution-protocol.md\" lines=\"1541\" start=\"1\" end=\"120\" returned=\"120\" has_more=\"true\" truncated=\"false\">\n     1|# Execution Protocol — Agent Runtime Standards\n     2|\n     3|## Purpose\n     4|\n     5|ROUTING: This file is self-routing. Read only the sections your skill needs,\n     6|in the order given by the routing rule below — do not preload the whole file.\n     7|Each skill discovers its sections from that rule, then reads each lazily as it\n     8|becomes relevant. Sections not selected for your skill are not required.\n     9|\n    10|This file defines how agent sessions manage identity, state, context limits,\n    11|recovery, section-by-section generation, and the harness output sidecar\n    12|contract. All code-development and synthesize skills follow these patterns;\n    13|the routing rule below tells you which sections apply to yours.\n    14|\n    15|---\n    16|\n    17|## Skill → Required Sections (routing rule)\n    18|\n    19|Skills self-route. Each SKILL.md declares its own **generation shape** inline\n    20|(`section-shape` = one multi-section spec; `list-shape` = many discrete files;\n    21|`hybrid` = section-shape manifest + list-shape per-item files) and references the\n    22|exact sections it applies at each step (`apply §10.2`, `§11`, …). Route by shape,\n    23|then layer on the role-based sections. Read in the listed order.\n    24|\n    25|**Base route by shape:**\n    26|- **section-shape / hybrid** → §2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11\n    27|- **list-shape** → §2 → §10.5 → §11 — produces many files, so §10 (section generation) does not apply.\n    28|\n    29|**Additive sections (layer onto the base route, in position):**\n    30|- **§1 (Session Identity)** — prepend for the code-development family, which mints/reuses a SESSION_ID in output filenames: `researching-code-design`, `planning-code-tasks`, `implementing-code`, `reviewing-code`, `reviewing-code-web`, `fixing-bugs`, `automation-code-generation`, `automation-test-execution`.\n    31|- **§12 (Delegated Exploration)** — insert right after §2 for skills that do broad read-only input sweeps: every `researching-*` skill, plus `reviewing-code`, `reviewing-code-web`, `planning-code-tasks`, `fixing-bugs`, `producing-abap-archaeology` (phases 1/2/4 sweep ABAP source exports), and `mapping-jde-fusion-data` (Phase B sweeps the JDE business-data KB and Oracle Fusion Documentation KB per in-scope table). Capability-gated and optional (see §12).\n    32|- **§13 (Code-Location Discipline)** — apply at the orientation / input-loading step for skills that locate code: every `researching-*` skill, plus `planning-code-tasks`, `implementing-code`, `reviewing-code`, `reviewing-code-web`, `fixing-bugs`, `conducting-excursion`, and the quick-lane `defining-quick-story` + `implementing-quick-change`. Read `codebase-map.md` before any repository-wide discovery scan (consult-before-scan, not never-scan; see §13). Composes with §12 — map first, then delegate the residual sweep.\n    33|- **§14 (Parallel Fan-Out)** — insert at the step that owns a same-shape independent-unit loop, for skills that declare an independent unit set. **Two axes decide the dispatch shape: unit independence (§14.1) and input shape (§14.1.1).** Routed skills, grouped by input shape:\n    34|  - **Disjoint inputs → one unit per worker.** `reviewing-code` (per changed file / diff-hunk group — own file + its direct imports/tests), `reviewing-code-web` (per changed frontend file / diff-hunk group — own file + its direct imports/tests, same shard rule as `reviewing-code`), `analyze-context-pack-inputs` (per input file — read-only extraction, coordinator synthesizes), `adversarial-platform-review` (per decision — each worker's own live lookup is the dominant cost), `db-source-profiling` (per source table — profile one table each; coordinator concats into the catalog), `db-parity-execution` (per table source↔target diff — the pass/fail verdict stays coordinator-serial; cap connection concurrency), `mapping-agent-tools` (per `TOOL-{NNN}` candidate), `planning-agent-backlog` (per agent candidate), `implementing-user-stories` + `generating-test-cases` + `generating-e2e-test-cases` (per epic — each suite/definition file derives only from that epic's own stories/journeys + ACs, so scope each worker's payload to its **epic slice**; handing a worker the whole PRD/architecture would convert this into a shared-corpus case), `researching-code-design` (per epic, Per-Epic Selective Loading Pattern ONLY — `implementation_sequencing` / `acceptance_criteria` sections, each worker reads one `epics/epic-{NN}.md` file (~60-130 lines) plus its own Tier-1 slice already resident in the coordinator's context; the OTHER 9 sections of that skill's spec are Tier-1-only rollups over a corpus every section shares and stay inline — routing those to §14 would reproduce the `validating-architecture-compliance` shared-corpus counter-example above), `secreview-static-scanning` (per (scanner, repo) pair — each pair writes its own finding files keyed by `finding_hash`; no pair reads another pair's output), `secreview-context-correlation` (per repo — each extraction reads only that repo's own source files and writes to its own `_extractions/repo-{name}.md`; no cross-repo reads occur during ext... (truncated)\n    35|  - **Shared corpus → batch-shard or run inline (§14.1.1).** `validating-architecture-compliance` (per story, but every story is judged against the same architecture + ADR + DTR corpus — the measured counter-example in §14.1.1: 8 workers 22 min vs inline 2 min 57 s; inline is now its default, batch-shard only at >= 12 stories), `generate-context-pack` (per normative context-pack file — all files derive from one upstream analysis digest; pass each worker its slice, batch-shard if no slice can be isolated).\n    36|  - **Mixed — the decorrelation exception covers only part of the unit set (§14.1.1).** `verifying-artifacts`: the whole checklist reads one shared `ARTIFACT_TEXT`, so **PROGRAM items (deterministic verifier runs) stay inline**, while **a JUDGE item's `judge_samples` draws DO fan out** — those samples must not see one another or `MEAN(samples)` stops being a variance-reduced score. Never one worker per checklist item: two different questions do not correlate, and bias-DOWN / anti-gaming are coordinator aggregation rules, not per-item isolation requirements. An all-PROGRAM checklist correctly fans out nothing.\n    37|\n    38|  Capability-gated and optional — the runtime advertises `Parallel delegation: AVAILABLE/UNAVAILABLE`. Concurrent foreground workers (N spawns in one dispatch turn) satisfy the capability; background/detached tasks are NOT required and an absent advertisement does NOT mean inline (§14 Applicability). Dispatch all workers for a shard in one turn (§14.3 step 2) — then **check the workers actually overlapped in time; if the harness serialized the batch, stop dispatching and finish the remainder inline (§14.7)**. Run inline in declared order when the harness is genuinely single-dispatch, or when §14.1.1 says the shared corpus makes inline the cheaper path (see §14). Write-capable sibling of §12: compose them (map/locate units via §12, process via §14). **NOT `implementing-code`** — parallel code writes to a shared tree require per-worker git-worktree isolation (not available to subagents by default), so its plan-phase loop stays inline until that isolation is guaranteed.\n    39|- **§15 (Deliverable Upload Safety)** — append at the finalize step, immediately BEFORE §11, for skills whose declared outputs include a `.html`, `.svg`, or standalone `.js` file delivered through the platform: `rapid-prototyping`, `sap-fiori-rapid-prototyping`, `generating-flow-selection-report`, `build-design-prototype`, `glob-presentation`, `producer-visualizing-architecture`, `producer-visualizing-story-map`. Those files are uploaded through a byte-scanning WAF that rejects attack-payload-shaped text — in code, in mock data, *or* in prose — before the request reaches the application, so a file that renders perfectly can still be undeliverable. §15.4 is a mandatory pre-sidecar scan. Not routed for markdown-only skills; see §15.4's closing note when a report may quote payloads.\n    40|\n    41|**Special routes (override the base route):**\n    42|- `implementing-code` → §1 → §2 → §3 (IMPL-STATE schema) → §5 (FIC monitoring) → §7 (if REPAIR) → §10.5 (+ §10.5.3 tool-output retention for build/test/lint runs) → §11\n    43|- `fixing-bugs` → §1 → §2 → §12 → §7 (if REPAIR) → §9 (non-interactive) → §10.5 → §11\n    44|- `refining-artifact-consistency` (sandbox) → §10.5 only — Stepwise owns `_progress.json` (§2) and apply-back (§11), so both are skipped; read §10.5 + §10.5.1 before any sandbox `Edit`.\n    45|- `conducting-excursion` (sandbox / Manual Bay) → §10.5 + §12 (capability-gated, narrowly — see below) only — Stepwise owns `_progress.json` (§2), the exit gate, and apply-back (§11), so both are skipped. Multi-turn: read the skill's working-set note (`EXCURSION-NOTES.md`) first on resume turns and do NOT re-run the §2/pre-flight read or re-scan source; read §10.5 + §10.5.1 before any sandbox `Edit`, and apply §10.5.3 (tool-output retention) to every build/test/deploy/probe run. §12 applies only to the rarer turn whose message is a genuinely broad sweep (e.g. tracing a symptom across the whole app, finding every place a config value is set) — the common turn that already names or implicates a single file stays inline per the skill's own minimum-evidence discipline (§12.1 already excludes single-known-file delegation).\n    46|- `authoring-deliverable` (Manual Bay / external-document authoring) → §10.5 (+ §10.5.1, + §10.5.3 for publish/render fetches) + §12 (capability-gated, narrowly) + §11 (declared outputs only) — Stepwise owns `_progress.json` (§2) and apply-back, so §2 and the lifecycle files are skipped; the deliverable is published to `document_target` through its own tool, and §11 reports the gate-consumed outputs (`authoring_status`, `document_url`, …) only. Multi-turn: read the working-set note (`AUTHORING-NOTES.md`) first on resume turns and do NOT re-run the §2/pre-flight read or re-fetch the published document to hunt for unrequested work; publish once and verify the render once (see the skill's `publish-and-render.md`). §12 applies only to the rarer turn whose message is a genuinely broad read-only sweep over many `source_artifacts` — a turn that names a single implicated file stays inline.\n    47|- `adversarial-platform-review` (discovery / conformance review) → §2 → §10.5 → §11 (+ §14 for per-decision fan-out) — list-shape: reads one bounded artifact + its declared target platform, adversarially web-verifies each load-bearing decision, and emits a single cited findings JSON + verdict (no multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply; no broad repo sweep, so §12 does not apply — the web grounding is this agent's own load-bearing work). Each load-bearing decision is an independent §14 unit (the coordinator merges + dedupes findings and decides the single verdict serially).\n    48|- `verifying-artifacts` (verification / checklist + program-verifier) → §2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 (+ §7 if REPAIR) (+ §14 for judge-sample fan-out) — section-shape: produces one multi-section VERIFICATION-REPORT (checklist → program_verifiers → judge_scoring → aggregation → verdict). Scores SPEC items + consumed `adversarial-platform-review` findings + a universal anti-gaming item; generates/runs stdlib-only verifier programs for exactly-checkable items (RLCF Fig 6) and mean-of-N judge-samples the rest. The per-item JUDGE samples (and independent PROGRAM runs) are the §14 fan-out unit — the coordinator does the mean/bias/anti-gaming aggregation serially. No broad repo sweep, so §12 does not apply; no platform re-discovery (that is the discovery layer's job).\n    49|- `triaging-lane` (loop-entry lane routing) → §2 → §10.5 → §11 — list-shape: reads ONE decomposition-manifest entry plus the relevant sections of `codebase-map.md` and the conformance ledger, answers a fixed seven-field evidence-cited questionnaire, and EXECUTES a versioned rule-table script to emit `FULL|QUICK`. No multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply. **§12 and §13 deliberately do NOT apply** — this step never scans or searches the repository, and delegating exploration would cost more than the lap it is routing; a field it cannot answer from those three inputs stays uncited and the item routes FULL. No §14 (a single work item is not a fan-out unit).\n    50|- `analyzing-pr-integration-order` (PR-batch merge-order analysis) → §2 → §10.5 → §11 — list-shape: discovers a batch of open PRs, detects explicit dependency mentions and file-overlap between them, and emits one decomposition JSON + one rationale report (no multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply). Read-only against the tracker/forge — no §12 (the reads are N bounded per-PR API calls, not a repo-wide sweep) and no §14 (a typical batch is small enough that per-PR metadata fetch does not warrant fan-out; revisit if batch sizes grow materially).\n    51|- `integrating-pull-requests` (per-PR conflict resolution + merge) → §2 → §10.5 → §11 — list-shape: invoked once per PR by `pr-integration`'s `integrate-prs` step, resolves a real merge conflict when one exists (NIMBLE auto-resolve / DEEP escalate) and executes the merge — no multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply. Given an explicit route rather than left to the catch-all: it writes real git state (test-merges, merge commits) under real time pressure from §16/AP-004-class mistakes documented in its own references, so its §11 sidecar and §10.5 tool discipline are load-bearing, not incidental. No §12 (git/API calls are bounded per-PR, not a repo-wide sweep) and no §14 (it IS the per-unit worker for the calling step's own loop, not a coordinator with units of its own to fan out).\n    52|- `generating-flow-selection-report` (prototype flow report) → §2 → §10.5 → §15 → §11 — list-shape: reads the agentic-mode `flow-documentation.md` + `component-inventory.md` of an already-built Fiori prototype and emits ONE self-contained `flow-report.html` INTO the prototype folder (beside `public/`), each screen deep-linking via iframe into the running local prototype. No multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply; no repo sweep, so §12 does not apply. §15 is load-bearing — the `.html` is delivered through the byte-scanning WAF, and it may transcribe screen titles/routes that must survive the pre-sidecar scan.\n    53|- `android-tc-agentic-execution` (Android TC → Maestro flow, device-driven) → §2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 (+ §4 opt-in) (+ §7 if REPAIR) — **hybrid**: the EXECUTION-JOURNAL is section-shape (ten sections, skeleton-first, one `Edit` per section), while the per-TC-step exploration records plus the flow YAML and `summary.md` are list-shape items with a seeded denominator. Apply §10.5.3 (tool-output retention) to every `maestro test`, `adb`, and emulator run, and to `report.html`. **§12 and §14 deliberately do NOT apply** — \"exploration\" here is stateful device interaction (driving a live emulator, mutating app state, capturing the current screen), not a read-only file sweep, so §12's `file:line` return contract cannot express it and a delegated session would race the one this agent depends on; and the TC steps are strictly ordered on a single shared device (step N+1's screen exists only because step N's action produced it), which is the strongest possible failure of §14's independence precondition. The one file-based sweep — skimming a prior `tests/*.yaml` for style — is a single known path and is read inline per §12.2.1.\n    54|- `planning-epics` (PRD → epic backlog, section-shape) → §2 → §12 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 (+ §7 if REPAIR) — section-shape: produces one multi-section EPICS-SPEC (skeleton-first, one `Edit` per section). §12 applies because the skill must load the full PRD-SPEC (potentially multi-file with batch files) before any FR-to-epic traceability claim can be made — a broad read-only sweep over unbounded input. No §14 (sections share the same PRD corpus and depend on prior-section output such as themes → epics ordering, so per-section fan-out would reproduce the shared-corpus counter-example).\n    55|- `plan-epics-definition` (requirements → epic backlog, standalone — no formal PRD required; section-shape) → §2 → §12 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 (+ §7 if REPAIR) — section-shape: produces one multi-section EPICS-SPEC (12 sections; 13 when a PRD-SPEC is auto-discovered) plus a metadata-only EPICS-AUDIT, skeleton-first with one `Edit` per section. §12 applies to the input-loading sweep, which has two parts, both read-only: (1) the PRD-SPEC auto-discovery in Step 1, **bounded to exactly two path patterns** — `PRD-SPEC-*.md` under `requirements_input_path` itself, then `./artifacts/outputs/*/prd/` — first search that returns any file wins; one match is read and feeds `prd_scope_validation`, more than one match is NOT merged (it becomes a single non-blocking `open_questions` row), none means standalone mode; (2) the Step 2 requirements ingestion over every file under `requirements_input_path`, which must be read in full before any FR-to-epic claim (never synthesize epics from a partial read). No §14 (sections share the same requirements/PRD corpus and later sections depend on earlier output — themes → epics → sequencing — so per-section fan-out would reproduce the shared-corpus counter-example).\n    56|- `analyzing-jde-fusion-scope` (JDE/Fusion scope extraction — Task 1.1 / Process Step 1 of `jde-fusion-integration-discovery`) → §2 → §10.5 → §11 — list-shape: reads the client-provided `input_document_path` once and extracts JDE Tools Release + implemented JDE modules + target Fusion modules/components into one `jde-fusion-scope-summary.md` (no multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply). Single bounded input read directly, not a broad sweep, so §12 does not apply. Single deliverable, no independent-unit loop, so §14 does not apply.\n    57|- `researching-fusion-integration-mechanisms` (JDE/Fusion mechanism research — Task 1.2 / Process Step 2 of `jde-fusion-integration-discovery`) → §2 → §12 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 — section-shape: produces one multi-section `integration-mechanism-research-findings.md` (skeleton-first, one `Edit` per section) evaluating REST/SOAP/FBDI per target Fusion module, RAG-grounded in the Oracle Fusion Documentation KB, JDE Orchestrator Guides KB, and the ErpIntegrationService reference documentation. §12 applies because the skill must broadly sweep three separate knowledge bases per module before any mechanism verdict can be cited — read-only exploration over an unbounded KB corpus. No §14 (the per-module evaluation loop shares the same 3-KB corpus across all modules — a shared-corpus shape per §14.1.1 — and module counts per scope are small, so fan-out was evaluated and rejected in favor of the inline loop).\n    58|- `specifying-integration-mechanism` (JDE-Fusion mechanism specification, section-shape) → §2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 — section-shape: produces one multi-section `integration-mechanism-specification.md` (mechanism_decision → rest_soap_specification → fbdi_specification → other_mechanism_specification → assumptions_to_validate → open_questions) from the bounded `researching-fusion-integration-mechanisms` output plus cited Oracle FBDI/`ErpIntegrationService` documentation. No broad repo/input sweep, so §12 does not apply; single mechanism decision drives generation, so no independent-unit loop and §14 does not apply.\n    59|- `mapping-jde-fusion-data` (JDE-to-Fusion field-level data mapping matrix) → §2 → §12 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 — section-shape: produces one multi-section Data Mapping Matrix (scope/mechanism context → methodology → per-table field mappings → unmapped fields and gaps → coverage summary → open questions), skeleton-first with one section per `Edit` call. §12 applies because Phase B must sweep the JDE business-data KB and Oracle Fusion Documentation KB per in-scope JDE table before any field mapping can be proposed — a broad read-only sweep over unbounded knowledge-base content when no client mapping file is supplied. No §14 (per-table generation shares the same mechanism_shape context across tables, so parallelizing them would reproduce the shared-corpus counter-example — tables stay coordinator-serial inside Phase C).\n    60|- `generating-jde-orchestrator-transformations` (JDE Orchestrator Groovy transformation code generation, Task 2.1) → §2 → §10.5 → §11 — list-shape: reads the `integration_mechanism_specification` and `data_mapping_matrix` produced by the discovery stream, classifies each mapping row as pass-through or custom-transform, and emits ONE terminal `groovy-transformation-scripts.md` report (no multi-section spec, so §10 does not apply; no broad repo/artifact sweep, so §12 does not apply; the per-row Groovy generation loop is bounded, structured pattern-matching, so no §14 fan-out).\n    61|- `generating-jde-orchestrator-configuration-guide` (JDE Orchestrator Configuration Guide generation) → §2 → §10 → §10.5 → §11 — section-shape: produces one multi-section `orchestrator-configuration-guide.md` (Connectors, Service Requests, Orchestrations, and cross-references), skeleton-first with one section per `Edit` call. Grounded in the Integration Mechanism Specification, Data Mapping Matrix, and Groovy transformation scripts from the discovery and build streams; for SOAP/FBDI mechanisms, also grounded in the Oracle ERP Integration whitepaper. No broad repo sweep, so §12 does not apply; single guide output, so §14 does not apply.\n    62|- `validating-erp-integration-reference` (ERP Integration Service reference pre-flight validation) → §2 → §11 — preflight shape: reads the Integration Mechanism Specification to detect the selected mechanism, checks erp_integration_service_reference_path readability for SOAP/FBDI mechanisms, emits PASS or BLOCKING. Writes one preflight sidecar; no deliverable artifact, no §10/§12/§14 sweep.\n    63|- **Catch-all** — any skill not otherwise classified → §2 → §10.5 → §11.\n    64|\n    65|Per-skill specifics that are NOT routing (e.g. MCP/Deterministic-API tooling, manifest-vs-per-file split, approval-token gates) live in each skill's own SKILL.md, not here.\n    66|\n    67|### Universal rules (apply to every skill, regardless of routing)\n    68|\n    69|A skill that discovers its sections by scanning for `## `/`### ` headers and\n    70|jumping straight to the ones its own route names by number will land on §2,\n    71|§10.5, §11 etc. directly — those have their own headers. It will NOT land on\n    72|this paragraph unless it is scanning headers, because until this heading was\n    73|added, this list had none: a header-jumping skill never learned these rules\n    74|existed at all, headers or not, since nothing pointed here. Observed\n    75|2026-09-04: an `opening-pull-request` run's own header-only section scan\n    76|(`grep -n \"^## §2\\|^## §10.5\\|^## §11\\|...\"`) skipped straight past this\n    77|paragraph and never discovered §16 — the very rule that would have stopped it\n    78|searching the filesystem for its own reference file. Read this list\n    79|unconditionally, not only when your route's header-scan happens to surface it.\n    80|\n    81|- §2 `_progress.json` FIRST ACTION / LAST ACTION write — prevents orchestrator SIGINT. **Exception:** sandbox-mode skills (e.g., `refining-artifact-consistency`, `conducting-excursion`) run inside a Stepwise-managed sandbox where Stepwise owns `_progress.json`; those skills skip §2 and use §10.5 only.\n    82|- §2.c path budget — at FIRST ACTION, measure the LONGEST path this run will write (your artifact filename, never `_progress.json`) and probe it once when it exceeds 240 characters. A successful `_progress.json` write is not evidence the output folder is usable: on Windows an over-long path surfaces as `ENOENT` and reads as a missing directory, and diagnosing that with a shell call is how a path error becomes a headless approval denial. Same exception as §2 (sandbox-mode skills).\n    83|- §10.5 tool discipline — applies whenever a skill writes or edits files, NOT only to section-shape skills. The destructive `edit` UTF-8 bug observed 2026-05-19 (zeroed out a target file on encoding failure with non-ASCII `new_string` content) is RESOLVED as of 2026-09-09 — see §10.5.1 for verification evidence. Targeted `Edit` is the path for every language now; no fallback needed.\n    84|- §11 harness output sidecar — last write before `final_response`. Without it the orchestrator records `outputs: {}` and downstream steps cannot pick up declared output parameters. **Exception:** sandbox-mode skills skip §11 because Stepwise owns the apply-back step; see the `refining-artifact-consistency` and `conducting-excursion` special routes above (`§10.5 only`).\n    85|- §11.6 open questions in the sidecar — `_open_questions` is a reserved, required row of the `## Output parameters` table: write it in the same sidecar as your declared outputs, listing every open question THIS run raised, `[]` when it raised nothing. Only questions THIS run raised: never re-list one the prompt handed you in `## OPEN QUESTIONS`, `## DECISIONS` or `## PROJECT DECISIONS`, in either place. Zero-Invention already tells you to raise an open question rather than guess (§9); this is what makes the raised question trackable instead of prose the orchestrator has to guess at. Same exception as §11.\n    86|- §4 Memory Bank session-end writes (`active-context.md`, `progress.md`) — only when the skill's SKILL.md explicitly opts in; otherwise skip.\n    87|- §7 REPAIR bookkeeping — when running in REPAIR mode (a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block is present), editing the artifact in place is NOT enough: you MUST also **bump the spec `version`** (semver patch, e.g. `1.0.0` → `1.0.1`) AND **record what changed in the AUDIT file** (set `mode: REPAIR` and append a `## Repair History` entry). Skipping this leaves the version and audit trail stale even though the content was fixed — the documented \"iteration applied but version/audit not updated\" defect. See §7.2 steps 7–8 for the exact fields and the mandatory self-check.\n    88|- §16 skill reference file resolution — whenever your own SKILL.md tells you to read a `references/*.md` file, resolve it relative to your own skill directory. Never `glob`/`find` the repo or filesystem to locate it. See §16 for why this hangs and how to recover.\n    89|- §12.6 / §13 both already forbid unscoped search **of source code under analysis**; §16 is the narrower, distinct rule for a skill's own bundled files and applies even to skills whose route omits §12/§13 entirely (e.g. `defining-quick-story`, list-shape/catch-all skills).\n    90|\n    91|**REPAIR mode override:** if the prompt contains a `## ⚠️ Repair feedback` block, read §7 (REPAIR Mode) before any other section — even when §7 is not listed in your route. §7 carries the version-bump + audit-delta bookkeeping that is mandatory for every REPAIR (see the universal rule above); the routing rule above intentionally omits it to stay short, so this override is the authoritative trigger.\n    92|\n    93|**REBUILD mode override (discard):** if the prompt contains a `## ♻️ Rebuild from scratch` block instead of `## ⚠️ Repair feedback`, read §7.3 (REBUILD Mode) before any other section. This is a DISCARD, not an iterate: the reviewer judged the artifact fundamentally wrong, so you must REGENERATE it from scratch (full BUILD) rather than surgically repair it — while still overwriting the SAME file/SESSION_ID (never duplicate) and doing the version-bump + audit bookkeeping (`mode: REBUILD`). REBUILD and REPAIR are mutually exclusive: only one of the two blocks is ever present.\n    94|\n    95|## Section Index\n    96|\n    97|- §1  — Session Identity (SESSION_ID format, REPAIR reuse rules)\n    98|- §2  — `_progress.json` early-signal write (FIRST ACTION / LAST ACTION)\n    99|  - §2.a Seed `total` once the item count is known (never leave `total: 0`)\n   100|  - §2.b Co-locate the tracker update with the checkpoint write in every production loop\n   101|  - §2.1 Pre-completion self-validation gate (universal — completeness · placeholders · grounding · format)\n   102|- §3  — IMPL-STATE schema (implementing-code only)\n   103|- §4  — Memory Bank cross-session continuity (`active-context.md`, `progress.md`)\n   104|- §5  — FIC context monitoring and recovery checkpoint\n   105|- §6  — Dual-format input detection\n   106|- §7  — REPAIR mode (when `failure_feedback` is provided); §7.3 — REBUILD mode (discard → regenerate)\n   107|- §8  — Deviation taxonomy (Auto-Fix / Escalate / Defer)\n   108|- §9  — Non-interactive execution rules\n   109|- §10 — Section-by-section spec generation (skeleton-first + one-section-per-write)\n   110|  - §10.1 Section-shape `_progress.json` schema\n   111|  - §10.2 Source extraction pass\n   112|  - §10.3 Phase A — Skeleton-first\n   113|  - §10.4 Phase B — Populate, one section at a time\n   114|  - §10.5 Tool discipline (hard rules; §10.5.1 non-ASCII fallback RETIRED — see below)\n   115|    - §10.5.3 Tool-output retention (context-budget: run full, retain only the verdict / failing slice / distilled evidence)\n   116|    - §10.5.4 Values you cannot compute — a checksum is RUN, never written from memory\n   117|  - §10.6 CONTINUE on re-entry\n   118|- §11 — Harness output sidecar (required when running under Stepwise)\n   119|  - §11.1.1 Path correctness (report the path you actually wrote to)\n   120|  - §11.1.2 Verdict fidelity (closed-set tokens verbatim; synonyms fail closed)\n</file>","isError":false,"durationMs":14}}
